1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,722
It’s only relatively recently that designers have become aware of the need to be

2
00:00:05,722 --> 00:00:07,790
inclusive when designing products.

3
00:00:07,790 --> 00:00:10,650
But what does that mean exactly?

4
00:00:10,650 --> 00:00:17,840
Well, it simply means designing products that span economic, social and cultural barriers.

5
00:00:17,840 --> 00:00:23,132
It means making sure products are accessible – so that as many different types

6
00:00:23,132 --> 00:00:28,423
of people as possible can use them without any type of adaptation having to

7
00:00:28,423 --> 00:00:31,470
be made to the original design.

8
00:00:31,470 --> 00:00:37,202
Inclusive design is often linked with universal design, although they are not quite the

9
00:00:37,202 --> 00:00:38,610
same thing.

10
00:00:38,610 --> 00:00:42,583
Universal design aims to make products that work for everyone

11
00:00:42,583 --> 00:00:47,739
and that includes considering the needs of people who have cognitive difficulties,

12
00:00:47,739 --> 00:00:51,030
which can present quite a challenge.

13
00:00:51,030 --> 00:00:57,450
Today, examples of successful inclusive design can be seen all around us.

14
00:00:57,450 --> 00:01:04,231
In workplaces it is common to see desks which can be adjusted to suit people of different heights

15
00:01:04,231 --> 00:01:07,020
or for wheelchair users.

16
00:01:07,020 --> 00:01:10,472
This still isn’t always the case, however,

17
00:01:10,472 --> 00:01:15,730
and is one reason why office workers often suffer from back or neck problems.

18
00:01:15,730 --> 00:01:22,047
You’ll find another example in the public toilets of countless hotels, airports

19
00:01:22,047 --> 00:01:30,310
and offices.Taps that you activate by sensor require no pressing or twisting movements.

20
00:01:30,310 --> 00:01:36,019
These are not only more hygienic, they’re also easier for people with dexterity

21
00:01:36,019 --> 00:01:38,110
or mobility issues.

22
00:01:38,110 --> 00:01:44,428
The tech industry has been criticised in the past for focusing too much on young consumers

23
00:01:44,428 --> 00:01:46,895
but this is changing.

24
00:01:46,895 --> 00:01:52,170
Many products are now designed with the elderly in mind.

25
00:01:52,170 --> 00:01:57,606
For example, it’s well known that vision declines with age,

26
00:01:57,606 --> 00:02:03,043
and that we also become worse at distinguishing between similar colours;

27
00:02:03,043 --> 00:02:05,514
in particular, shades of blue,

28
00:02:05,514 --> 00:02:10,950
which is why software designers rarely create interfaces with this colour.

29
00:02:10,950 --> 00:02:19,327
Motor skills also decline with age and some people have difficulty doing everyday things like picking up a cup

30
00:02:19,327 --> 00:02:21,090
or opening a door.

31
00:02:21,090 --> 00:02:26,631
This can also affect their ability to use a mouse or keyboard.

32
00:02:26,631 --> 00:02:31,710
So voice access is now a routine way of making commands.

33
00:02:31,710 --> 00:02:37,888
It’s worth looking at the problems non-inclusive designs cause when not enough consideration is

34
00:02:37,888 --> 00:02:43,654
given to a range of users, as it can have a serious impact on

35
00:02:43,654 --> 00:02:44,890
people’s lives.

36
00:02:44,890 --> 00:02:52,154
Access is one obvious example because it has such a huge impact on disabled

37
00:02:52,154 --> 00:02:53,710
people’s independence.

38
00:02:53,710 --> 00:03:00,466
Not being able to access public transport because buses or trains are not wheelchair-friendly

39
00:03:00,466 --> 00:03:06,740
means many disabled people can’t go out unless someone goes with them.

40
00:03:06,740 --> 00:03:09,062
Safety is another issue.

41
00:03:09,062 --> 00:03:17,190
Inexplicably, most cars are still crash-tested using a dummy based on an average-sized male.

42
00:03:17,190 --> 00:03:23,837
This has safety implications for all women, particularly those who are pregnant, as the

43
00:03:23,837 --> 00:03:29,060
seatbelts worn by the dummy are not adapted to accommodate them.

44
00:03:29,060 --> 00:03:36,489
Over the past 100 years, workplaces in the UK have, on the whole, become

45
00:03:36,489 --> 00:03:37,550
considerably safer.

46
00:03:37,550 --> 00:03:42,543
Employers are legally required to provide well-maintained personal protective equipment

47
00:03:42,543 --> 00:03:48,534
or PPE – anything from goggles to full body suits – to workers who

48
00:03:48,534 --> 00:03:51,030
need it, free of charge.

49
00:03:51,030 --> 00:03:54,266
But most PPE is designed to fit men.

50
00:03:54,266 --> 00:03:59,928
A recent report found that employers often think that when it comes to female

51
00:03:59,928 --> 00:04:05,590
workers, all they need to do to comply with this legal requirement is to

52
00:04:05,590 --> 00:04:09,230
buy jackets, for example, designed for a small man.

53
00:04:09,230 --> 00:04:14,059
The problem with this is that women can be tall

54
00:04:14,059 --> 00:04:18,887
and still have much smaller shoulders than the average man.

55
00:04:18,887 --> 00:04:25,647
Ill-fitting PPE such as hi-vis jackets, vests and body armour can put women at

56
00:04:25,647 --> 00:04:26,130
risk.

57
00:04:26,130 --> 00:04:33,544
The report found that 95% of women said that their PPE often hampered their work

58
00:04:33,544 --> 00:04:39,970
and that this problem was worst in the emergency services, particularly the police.

59
00:04:39,970 --> 00:04:43,930
Another problem is related to comfort at work.

60
00:04:43,930 --> 00:04:50,530
A very common scenario in offices in summertime is to see women wrapped in blankets

61
00:04:50,530 --> 00:04:54,930
or wearing sweaters while the air conditioning is on high.

62
00:04:54,930 --> 00:05:02,796
Meanwhile the men are in shorts and T-shirts.This is due to differences in metabolic rates for men

63
00:05:02,796 --> 00:05:03,670
and women.

64
00:05:03,670 --> 00:05:09,062
There is a standard setting for air conditioning to be at a temperature of

65
00:05:09,062 --> 00:05:11,373
21 degrees, designed to suit men,

66
00:05:11,373 --> 00:05:17,150
and in most modern offices it’s not possible to turn the air conditioning up

67
00:05:17,150 --> 00:05:17,920
or down.

68
00:05:17,920 --> 00:05:24,780
This means that many offices which mainly employ women are wasting energy by having

69
00:05:24,780 --> 00:05:27,720
the air conditioning set too high.

70
00:05:27,720 --> 00:05:32,922
As you can see from the examples I’ve just mentioned, there are serious consequences

71
00:05:32,922 --> 00:05:36,736
for designs which don’t consider the needs of all users.

72
00:05:36,736 --> 00:05:37,430
This is…
